  # Blog Post 1 - Svelte 5
  - ::
    id: "screen-post1"
    name: "Post: Svelte 5 Runes"
    css: "background: #f7fafc; padding: 40px; min-height: 100vh;"
    children::
      - ::
        type: "nav-button"
        content: "← Back to Home"
        action: "navigate"
        targetContainerId: "screen-home"
        css: "background: #e2e8f0; color: #2d3748; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 30px; border: none; cursor: pointer;"

      - ::
        type: "section-container"
        name: "Article Container"
        css: "max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);"
        children::
          - ::
            type: "heading"
            content: "Getting Started with Svelte 5 Runes"
            css: "color: #1a202c; font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 10px; font-weight: 800;"

          - ::
            type: "text"
            content: "📅 January 15, 2025  •  By Sarah Chen  •  ⏱️ 5 min read"
            css: "color: #718096; margin-bottom: 30px;"

          - ::
            type: "markdown-text"
            content: "Svelte 5 introduces **Runes**, a revolutionary approach to reactivity that makes your code more explicit and easier to understand.\n\n## What are Runes?\n\nRunes are special symbols that tell Svelte how to handle reactivity:\n\n- `$state` - Create reactive state\n- `$derived` - Compute derived values\n- `$effect` - Run side effects\n- `$props` - Declare component props\n\n## Example: Counter Component\n\n```svelte\n<script>\n  let count = $state(0);\n  let doubled = $derived(count * 2);\n  \n  $effect(() => {\n    console.log('Count changed:', count);\n  });\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={() => count++}>\n  Count: {count} (doubled: {doubled})\n</button>\n```\n\n## Why Runes?\n\nRunes make reactivity **explicit** rather than implicit. You know exactly what's reactive and what's not. This leads to:\n\n✅ Better performance  \n✅ Easier debugging  \n✅ More predictable behavior  \n✅ Better TypeScript support\n\nTry them out in your next project!"
            mode: "markdown"
            css: "line-height: 1.8; color: #2d3748; margin-bottom: 40px;"

          - ::
            type: "heading"
            content: "💬 Comments & Discussion"
            css: "color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px; margin-top: 40px; padding-top: 40px; border-top: 2px solid #e2e8f0;"

          - ::
            type: "thread"
            name: "Post 1 Comments"
            mode: "markdown"
            description: "Share your thoughts about Svelte 5 Runes"
            css: "min-height: 400px;"

  # Blog Post 2 - Yjs
  - ::
    id: "screen-post2"
    name: "Post: Yjs Collaboration"
    css: "background: #f7fafc; padding: 40px; min-height: 100vh;"
    children::
      - ::
        type: "nav-button"
        content: "← Back to Home"
        action: "navigate"
        targetContainerId: "screen-home"
        css: "background: #e2e8f0; color: #2d3748; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 30px; border: none; cursor: pointer;"

      - ::
        type: "section-container"
        name: "Article Container"
        css: "max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);"
        children::
          - ::
            type: "heading"
            content: "Understanding Yjs for Real-time Collaboration"
            css: "color: #1a202c; font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 10px; font-weight: 800;"

          - ::
            type: "text"
            content: "📅 January 18, 2025  •  By Alex Rivera  •  ⏱️ 8 min read"
            css: "color: #718096; margin-bottom: 30px;"

          - ::
            type: "markdown-text"
            content: "Building real-time collaborative applications is challenging. **Yjs** makes it simple by providing conflict-free replicated data types (CRDTs).\n\n## What is Yjs?\n\nYjs is a high-performance CRDT implementation that enables:\n\n- **Conflict-free merging** - Multiple users can edit simultaneously\n- **Offline support** - Changes sync when connection restores  \n- **Undo/Redo** - Full history management\n- **Small updates** - Efficient binary protocol\n\n## Basic Example\n\n```javascript\nimport * as Y from 'yjs';\n\n// Create a shared document\nconst ydoc = new Y.Doc();\n\n// Get a shared map\nconst blocks = ydoc.getMap('blocks');\n\n// Listen for changes\nblocks.observe((event) => {\n  console.log('Blocks updated:', event.changes);\n});\n\n// Update data (syncs to all peers)\nblocks.set('block1', {\n  type: 'text',\n  content: 'Hello, world!'\n});\n```\n\n## Use Cases\n\n🎨 **Collaborative Editors** - Google Docs-style editing  \n📊 **Shared Whiteboards** - Miro, Figma-like tools  \n🎮 **Multiplayer Games** - Real-time game state  \n📝 **Form Builders** - Like what we're building!\n\n## Architecture\n\nYjs works with a simple architecture:\n\n1. Each client has a `Y.Doc` instance\n2. Changes create update messages (Uint8Array)\n3. Updates are sent to other clients via WebSocket/WebRTC\n4. Yjs automatically merges updates conflict-free\n\nNo central server needed for conflict resolution!"
            mode: "markdown"
            css: "line-height: 1.8; color: #2d3748; margin-bottom: 40px;"

          - ::
            type: "heading"
            content: "💬 Comments & Discussion"
            css: "color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px; margin-top: 40px; padding-top: 40px; border-top: 2px solid #e2e8f0;"

          - ::
            type: "thread"
            name: "Post 2 Comments"
            mode: "markdown"
            description: "Discuss real-time collaboration and Yjs"
            css: "min-height: 400px;"

  # Blog Post 3 - HUML
  - ::
    id: "screen-post3"
    name: "Post: HUML Language"
    css: "background: #f7fafc; padding: 40px; min-height: 100vh;"
    children::
      - ::
        type: "nav-button"
        content: "← Back to Home"
        action: "navigate"
        targetContainerId: "screen-home"
        css: "background: #e2e8f0; color: #2d3748; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 30px; border: none; cursor: pointer;"

      - ::
        type: "section-container"
        name: "Article Container"
        css: "max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);"
        children::
          - ::
            type: "heading"
            content: "HUML: A Declarative UI Markup Language"
            css: "color: #1a202c; font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 10px; font-weight: 800;"

          - ::
            type: "text"
            content: "📅 January 20, 2025  •  By Jamie Lee  •  ⏱️ 6 min read"
            css: "color: #718096; margin-bottom: 30px;"

          - ::
            type: "markdown-text"
            content: "**HUML** (Hierarchical UI Markup Language) is a declarative format for defining complete applications, from simple websites to complex forms and branching flows.\n\n## Why HUML?\n\nTraditional UI development requires:\n- Writing component code\n- Managing state\n- Handling routing\n- Styling components\n\nHUML lets you **declare** the entire structure in one place using YAML-like syntax.\n\n## Key Features\n\n### 📦 Complete Application Definition\nDefine screens, blocks, navigation, forms, and styling in one file.\n\n### 🎨 CSS Support\nInline CSS for custom styling:\n```huml\ncss: \"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%);\"\n```\n\n### 🔀 Navigation & Forms\n- Simple navigation between screens\n- Form fields with validation\n- Branching logic with field values\n\n### 💬 Interactive Elements\n- Thread blocks for comments\n- Form submissions\n- Real-time collaboration\n\n## Real-World Example\n\nThis entire blog you're reading was defined in HUML! Check out the template to see how:\n\n- Multiple blog post screens\n- Navigation between posts\n- Markdown content rendering\n- Thread comments at the bottom\n- Custom styling throughout\n\nHUML makes it incredibly easy to prototype and build complex UIs declaratively."
            mode: "markdown"
            css: "line-height: 1.8; color: #2d3748; margin-bottom: 40px;"

          - ::
            type: "heading"
            content: "💬 Comments & Discussion"
            css: "color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px; margin-top: 40px; padding-top: 40px; border-top: 2px solid #e2e8f0;"

          - ::
            type: "thread"
            name: "Post 3 Comments"
            mode: "markdown"
            description: "What do you think about HUML?"
            css: "min-height: 400px;"

  # About Page
  - ::
